#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124516 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "87580982-1d5a-5f68-b9c8-df77b4da0196" :question/math-academy-id "e-5439" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/3d/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](images/5f/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](images/f9/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](images/ca/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f3694e9e-804f-55f0-9fb1-a78caec29224" :question/math-academy-id "e-5440" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation ![](images/a4/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124327 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "fc0725a9-354c-5e01-a924-12c0d2723ba3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5441" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/d0/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](images/4d/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5ba5ded2-bfca-5e53-9056-8aede726505f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5616" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n![](images/c3/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](images/4f/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070baad4-ad33-576e-8c10-a318d6e24cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269332} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0e050-f330-5a1f-bcc4-cb34b06b865a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270096} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea1a9bf-b113-5ae1-91d8-81b5bf35f9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270107} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfb5ce2-72e9-5f83-a46c-2cb7f06d73d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270656} {:answer/id #uuid "e28386a4-5eee-51eb-a73b-54fd86410c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea1a9bf-b113-5ae1-91d8-81b5bf35f9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b615d50c-14ad-5847-9c65-aea5729ff569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14df7021-329f-5b79-9249-773f1affa3e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139998" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y \\\\ 6x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is ${v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124516 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe092bb-bdb8-550c-a7aa-700202769f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419555} {:answer/id #uuid "34464ede-fe32-54e3-8700-7eae73a44d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-4z \\\\ y \\\\ 2z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419556} {:answer/id #uuid "5e442e08-651b-596c-a28f-613f716517a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419562} {:answer/id #uuid "87b1ab8b-53cc-5796-820a-719dc0cbb1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4x+2y\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419567} {:answer/id #uuid "f2706581-c2a4-529c-8a29-695dc8229666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 4x+2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe092bb-bdb8-550c-a7aa-700202769f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419555} :answer-field/id #uuid "07ed2ad4-a2fd-5d04-be73-f4b3ba6ae7db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b7b7cc-73cb-5678-9200-bd4242fb8265" :question/math-academy-id "q-140056" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z \\\\ -3x-6y+3z \\\\ 4x+8y-4z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y+z.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6d2ee1-42d5-5a7f-822c-94ea137fe0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364970} {:answer/id #uuid "4aecd48f-f9da-5257-8263-d68bbc2b3da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364971} {:answer/id #uuid "8db28dbb-a466-5172-a6eb-ac3463671499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364972} {:answer/id #uuid "67f0e235-17a6-5c48-97cf-566fdd5f365e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364973} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d35d2d-b74b-5c8c-bb4e-394ac2ab72c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db28dbb-a466-5172-a6eb-ac3463671499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364972} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1a7aa9-c34f-5290-9a38-a5a184cf9364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c099173-4895-55c8-a699-8c50ffc5eadb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140066" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation\n $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-x-4z \\\\ 2x+7z-6y \\\\ 16z+4x-12y\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the 2nd one).  Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3542d1a9-0149-5a87-845a-c2a1358d7505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321555} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7dfa2f-34db-56d9-a8fb-ff799a2f2948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321879} {:answer/id #uuid "894064c2-fa57-59f6-a23e-da4997fa484f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322241} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb5a7b2-df0c-52da-ad36-b5e249253134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322737} {:answer/id #uuid "d84d5ce8-ec22-54a6-9429-dcd6dbbd5b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3542d1a9-0149-5a87-845a-c2a1358d7505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321555} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53b2e36-9418-53bf-a391-31c49bfdb506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2204189b-4707-5bbc-8475-ce3acaaee8eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37712" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{aligned}]$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502cb7bf-d839-54d5-80e3-9ea7df778ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321776} {:answer/id #uuid "8159cef6-c1a1-590b-96b6-466ca0ae35d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322166} {:answer/id #uuid "a12a2a6a-d3e6-5f9c-85e7-7b997b09b179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322419} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f135c2-1de9-5952-8c6b-92b9bf7a9ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322422} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cf9c20-5a3a-56ff-ad42-b615924be6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9cf9c20-5a3a-56ff-ad42-b615924be6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322481} :answer-field/id #uuid "df87c177-5600-5668-aa22-f776774133ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c08047d4-78e2-5052-a86f-f3f6f58c0260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37825" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -10x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ -10\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4k-12 \\\\ -10k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4k-12=0 \\\\ -10k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124516 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26744c84-4da4-549b-b265-e365ea1b2c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321446} {:answer/id #uuid "5225c40a-0242-5a8f-b880-4361e75f2a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321795} {:answer/id #uuid "835ce376-7ad0-5448-89b0-5c53120894fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322182} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf0275-58df-5691-a4af-730ecca8c70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322552} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb2b8a5-0b68-56a8-bbf3-314fbb8d0d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5225c40a-0242-5a8f-b880-4361e75f2a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321795} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7e412b-267f-58a3-94eb-d3421d219bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d0e70ad-1c81-5568-a326-e79afb6bb189" :question/math-academy-id "q-37827" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124516 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a60cd4e9-d478-52ef-871e-fb08ff3d68a5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ada205dd-671e-513b-a239-bff737f60096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322507} {:answer/id #uuid "c880ad5e-41b7-50c0-ad0e-0f3ceca5661f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322702} {:answer/id #uuid "ca358ed5-3365-58e9-b485-afbd43139c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322716} {:answer/id #uuid "cc264154-95a2-5c1e-ae08-7da5839442b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322735} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dfad8e-a4ec-513e-9adf-6d6cb3f481cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca358ed5-3365-58e9-b485-afbd43139c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322716} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c15bcb8-d6d5-5caf-9815-06b85b3af955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1546ae0c-0405-54cb-8c7c-cf6c58a51fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-37828" :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14eed407-6b25-5868-ba78-53d73bba205f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366080} {:answer/id #uuid "48f9467e-1f3a-5b33-ad4e-9ce3ae6d192c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366081} {:answer/id #uuid "2eca51ec-bc3d-50c3-9c4d-8fc7d0ace6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366082} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc4202d-ea22-57a4-8d4c-07df0c1cd24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366083} {:answer/id #uuid "1341a9b7-41b2-5380-b684-2557cf69533c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1341a9b7-41b2-5380-b684-2557cf69533c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366084} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e35217a-c5a8-5016-8924-bddec15e8b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e24e150-5788-5770-a2a9-706c3d3592ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-38645" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ 4y+3x\\end{aligned}],$ find  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1=2\\cdot 0-1=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2731d7-9bf4-59e2-a5fd-0d22940726ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366360} {:answer/id #uuid "709deace-e72b-54bb-b36d-e6919d9318f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366361} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e702e-0eca-5685-a172-661e4405d724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366362} {:answer/id #uuid "bc073656-5139-5cae-8a20-3b10563f43e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366363} {:answer/id #uuid "c03c8cfa-dd96-52a1-98a2-b3436e3bfde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc073656-5139-5cae-8a20-3b10563f43e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366363} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2a5091-37af-5723-bdf8-06f99b82a475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b7bb489-080b-516a-993a-be6a97310491" :question/math-academy-id "q-80667" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}10x+6y \\\\ 25x+15y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot k+6\\cdot 5 \\\\ 25\\cdot k+15\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10k+30 \\\\ 25k+75\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}10k+30=0 \\\\ 25k+75=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f10884b6-1de6-5d92-bda1-96200aef78a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394039} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cbfff0-9403-5eeb-ab47-cfb10e097206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394040} {:answer/id #uuid "17e21db7-2740-54eb-87ab-43f4c7023e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394041} {:answer/id #uuid "656d0480-66ba-51ee-b9a6-82fc1fbfd015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394042} {:answer/id #uuid "298f458f-dd8f-56ee-86d2-8a73d3189728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2cbfff0-9403-5eeb-ab47-cfb10e097206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394040} :answer-field/id #uuid "92b7acaf-3ad9-5680-910b-7d4f59738910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4dce28d-b322-5c7f-88fd-a0c01e48564e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80671" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y+3z \\\\ 2x+y+z \\\\ x-y+5z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (-2)+3+3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+3+k \\\\ -2-3+5\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3+3k \\\\ -1+k \\\\ -5+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3+3k=0 \\\\ -1+k=0 \\\\ -5+5k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} {:answer/id #uuid "15868a01-ce63-5e6b-b375-fec48a6e6ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404180} {:answer/id #uuid "55de43a8-0397-50c5-867b-d34348940323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404181} {:answer/id #uuid "5c165536-c143-5156-8063-ecfdc1329b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404182} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1de765-7e1f-51a4-a847-e7a575a67eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124518 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d9208e7-9ee2-5220-9d62-6259432913d9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Component of a Vector in the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065fe2d2-27bc-5b0d-a756-2e96d9074922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348504} {:answer/id #uuid "2d10ef55-04cd-5945-9adf-137539253155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348842} {:answer/id #uuid "98939c03-5823-5e95-9652-7698824603e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349812} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9417f4-4771-5587-8a64-bfea64370050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349968} {:answer/id #uuid "dea26071-f0ca-55a2-8485-1f20e0bdf83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065fe2d2-27bc-5b0d-a756-2e96d9074922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348504} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c54f7c-daab-59c3-8960-8e6c25c2f170" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "328b0afd-0c82-5cff-b5a3-0cdd36c80b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-81387" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot k-4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3k+12 \\\\ 2k-8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3k+12=0 \\\\ 2k-8=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e0f2a4d-8be3-5c94-a0eb-8fa431f10e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349011} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2ad5e9-80f9-59d3-bf0a-5772922b7eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349972} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a61828-1c65-5971-b508-db09e73a25a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350224} {:answer/id #uuid "eadf86d8-8cac-5bc5-8c8a-f406c5cf7f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350499} {:answer/id #uuid "eecfcb3a-e593-5747-8eed-cc88c924c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9a61828-1c65-5971-b508-db09e73a25a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350224} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d71717-e8b9-5e76-acae-e00cc687fe36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae878865-22de-5344-874b-fc28f0188962" :question/math-academy-id "q-81391" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 16y-6x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3=2\\cdot 1-3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124517 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d2fde0-af1d-5e87-be58-806b9d444144" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53750d24-755f-51fd-b4b1-3999616bb4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349208} {:answer/id #uuid "54c76c9c-3dfe-5f84-83f4-73a8745d7382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349221} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdfe70d-a6a8-5c14-b42f-ef3b4b5f1d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349293} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdc1432-e609-5d49-8860-54ee3461005e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349719} {:answer/id #uuid "996f8b5f-1dc0-553a-9347-7d0ebc42480f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53750d24-755f-51fd-b4b1-3999616bb4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349208} :answer-field/id #uuid "143a07e8-1e6a-5468-9248-cfae98764d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3e25176-30ac-5743-87ee-ef074c94ef6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81392" :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$"}]}
